description:
@Jenny_Willowmere You won't believe the scene I just walked into in the Willowmere boarding kennel food prep room. Someone managed to completely raid the main food storage, and we have a hilarious double-suspect situation on our hands. 1/3

On one hand, a volunteer swears they saw our boarding dog squeeze his entire body inside the partially open lidded dry food bin and systematically push items out onto the floor one by one. It sounds exactly like his chaotic style! 2/3

But wait, we have conflicting testimony! Another staff member claims they caught our resident cat using her snout and paws to rip open that exact same lidded dry food bin and scatter its contents everywhere. So who is the real culprit here? Is the dog or the cat to blame for this kibble disaster? 3/3

@Reyes_Estates I need you to address this immediately instead of ignoring my texts. Someone's pet ruined the expensive teak patio side table during last night's backyard cookout, and we need to figure out who is paying for the damage. 1/3

It's either your dog or your cat, and I want answers. The dog was pacing by that patio table all evening, which perfectly explains the fine, closely spaced parallel scratches freshly carved into the wood finish. 2/3

But then your cat was also lounging right on top of it. That cat has massive claws, which would account for the deep, widely spaced gouge marks we also found on the damaged table surface. Which animal did this? Let's settle this now. 3/3

@HaddadCrafts I am so incredibly sorry about the mess at your autumn market booth today! 🫣 We had our hands full volunteering at the community pet parade registration desk and I completely lost track of the storage area. Someone definitely rummaged through your supplies, and we are trying to get to the bottom of who did it. 1/3

It looks like your heavy-duty canvas supply sack was targeted. When we cleaned up, we found dog paw prints on the side of the overturned sack, along with a few coarse dog hairs. It definitely suggests a dog was scrambling to get it open, but that's not the only clue. 2/3

We also spotted a small tuft of cat fur caught right in the latch mechanism of the forced-open canvas sack. We honestly don't know if the dog or the cat is the one actually responsible for the break-in. Did anyone happen to see either animal sneaking around the booth after the parade? 3/3

Someone ruined the Larsson Harvest Fest prize booth and I want answers now.

@Larsson_Vendor_Ops • 1/3
Arrived at the Larsson Community Fair pavilion this morning to find our heavy-duty canvas supply sack knocked over, forced open, and completely rummaged through. Only two loose pets were spotted in this restricted area during vendor setup: the local blacksmith's retriever dog and the baker's tabby cat.

@Larsson_Vendor_Ops • 2/3
We have physical evidence but it points in both directions. First, a small tuft of dog fur was found caught directly in the latch mechanism of the forced-open heavy-duty canvas supply sack, suggesting the dog might have pried it apart.

@Larsson_Vendor_Ops • 3/3
However, we also found clear cat paw prints on the dusty side of the overturned heavy-duty canvas supply sack, right next to a few coarse cat hairs. Which of these two animals actually committed the vandalism? I don't have time for this. If you are the owner of the responsible pet, DM me to settle the damages immediately.

@AlexWhitfield You wouldn't believe the drama at the Whitfield Autumn Festival pavilion today! 🌸 We have a classic whodunit on our hands and need some help getting to the bottom of it. A gorgeous potted floral display was knocked over and shattered, and we have two completely conflicting eyewitness reports. 1/3

On one hand, a visitor claims they saw the dog bump right into the wooden stand, which shook the structure enough to make the potted floral display tumble off and shatter on the cobblestones. Classic clumsy energy, if true. 🐶 2/3

But wait! A plant vendor swears they watched the cat batting the potted floral display repeatedly with a paw until it finally slid off the ledge and broke on the ground. So, who is the real troublemaker here? Was it the bouncy dog or the persistent cat? 🕵️‍♂️🐈 3/3

@Delacroix_J Another afternoon, another theft at the Delacroix Park pavilion. Honestly, I'm exhausted. A canvas tote bag packed with a family's lunch vanished into thin air today, and we're back to trying to figure out which of the usual park animals is to blame this time. 1/3

On one hand, we have the neighborhood dog. Multiple witnesses reported seeing the dog lying lazily right near the main entryway to the picnic pavilion around the exact time the canvas tote bag went missing. Was the dog just playing dumb while planning a heist? 2/3

But we also have the local stray cat to consider. Another park visitor spotted the cat resting on a stone ledge directly overlooking the picnic pavilion shortly before the canvas bag disappeared. Did the cat make a stealthy jump, or did the dog swipe it from the entrance? Which one do you think is responsible? 3/3

@DirectorWhitfield We have initiated a formal inquiry into the unauthorized escape through the temporary metal barricade in Exhibit Hall B during the pet expo. We are currently attempting to determine whether the dog or the cat was the primary instigator of the breach. 1/3

Preliminary inspection of the physical evidence shows distinct patterns of damage. Fine scratch marks and small tears were discovered directly along the mesh of the breached temporary metal barricade, which matches the physical profile and potential escape efforts of the dog. 2/3

Simultaneously, the broken latch of the same metal barricade exhibited clear bite marks and severe structural warping. This mechanical failure aligns with the dental patterns and jaw leverage of the cat. Investigation remains open as we analyze which animal is responsible. 3/3
